research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11061-1.2 Assuming [A2], the space $\omega_1$ with its order topology is countably compact by [L1], and the closed long ray $R$ is countably compact by [L2]. [A2, L1, L2]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11062-**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** For a metric space the
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11063-two conditions are equivalent, and that equivalence is proved elsewhere in this
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log:11064:library at a stated choice cost; the claim above is that equivalence transplanted
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11065-to an arbitrary topological space, where it fails. What does hold in general is
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11066-only that sequential compactness implies countable compactness, and that at the
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-t1-compactness-core.log-11067-cost of countable choice.
