research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8559-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:39:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8560-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:40:**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** For a metric space the
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8561-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:41:two conditions are equivalent, and that equivalence is proved elsewhere in this
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8562:items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:42:library at a stated choice cost; the claim above is that equivalence transplanted
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8563-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:43:to an arbitrary topological space, where it fails. What does hold in general is
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8564-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:44:only that sequential compactness implies countable compactness, and that at the
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-8565-items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md:45:cost of countable choice.
