17-
18:**Objective.** Urysohn's lemma separates two disjoint closed sets of a normal
19:space by a continuous real-valued function; Tietze's extension theorem
20-extends a continuous function on a closed subspace of a normal space to the
21-whole space. This page proves both, under the Axiom of Dependent Choice, and
22-develops the mechanism each proof shares: a family of open sets indexed by the
23-dyadic rationals of $[0,1]$, nested by closure, defines the separating or
24-extending function as an infimum.
25-
26-**The mechanism.** [[def-the-dyadic-rationals-of-the-unit-interval]] fixes the
27-dyadic rationals of $[0,1]$ level by level and proves their density.
28-[[lem-a-dyadic-scale-of-open-sets-defines-a-continuous-function]] shows,
29:without any choice principle, that a family of open sets indexed by those
30-dyadics with closures nested inside the next member defines a continuous map
31:into $[0,1]$; every choice-consuming step of the page happens earlier, in
32-building such a family, never in this lemma.
33-
34:**Urysohn's lemma and its converse.** [[thm-urysohn-lemma]] builds a nested
35:dyadic family by dependent choice and proves that a normal space's disjoint
36-closed sets are separated by a continuous function into $[0,1]$; it also
37-proves the converse, that a space with this separation property is normal,
38:with no choice principle. [[cor-urysohns-lemma-closes-the-separation-chain]]
39-applies the lemma to a point and a closed set in a normal $T_1$ space,
40-supplying the arrow $T_4 \Rightarrow T_{3\frac12}$ and assembling it with the
41-implications already proved elsewhere into the full classical separation
42-chain.
43-
44:**Tietze's extension theorem.** [[lem-a-uniformly-approximable-real-valued-map-is-continuous]]
45-proves that a real-valued function approximable to any tolerance by a
46-continuous function is itself continuous, and in particular that a series of
47-continuous functions dominated termwise by a convergent series of constants
48-has a continuous sum. [[thm-a-space-is-perfectly-normal-iff-it-is-normal-and-every-closed-set-is-a-zero-set]]
49:combines this with Urysohn's lemma to characterise perfect normality: a
50-normal space is perfectly normal exactly when every closed set is the zero
51-set of a continuous function. [[thm-tietze-extension-theorem]] combines it
52:with a geometrically decaying series of Urysohn functions to extend a
53-continuous map on a closed subspace into $[a,b]$, and proves the converse:
54-the extension property characterises normality.
55-[[cor-tietze-for-unbounded-and-open-interval-valued-maps]] widens the target
56-from a closed bounded interval to $\mathbb{R}$ and to an open interval,
57-composing the bounded case with an explicit homeomorphism.
58-
59-**Compactness and complete regularity.** [[thm-a-locally-compact-hausdorff-space-is-completely-regular]]
60:applies Urysohn's lemma inside the one-point compactification of a locally
61-compact Hausdorff space to show it is completely regular, hence Tychonoff.
62-[[cor-a-compact-hausdorff-space-is-tychonoff]] records the compact Hausdorff
63:case directly, together with Urysohn separation for its own disjoint closed
64-sets.
65-
66:**Choice cost.** [[rem-the-choice-cost-of-urysohns-lemma-and-of-tietzes-theorem]]
67:accounts for where each theorem on this page spends dependent choice, where
68-the perfect-normality theorem separately performs a step shaped like
69:countable choice and discharges it as an instance of dependent choice, and
70-which results — the dyadic-scale lemma, the $M$-test, and the metric case of
71:every theorem here — use no choice principle at all.
72-
73-**False statements** mark the boundary of what normality alone supplies.
74-[[fs-every-normal-space-is-completely-regular]] refutes normality without
75-$T_1$ implying complete regularity, using Sierpinski space.
76-[[fs-a-continuous-real-function-on-any-subspace-of-a-normal-space-extends]]
77-refutes the extension property for a subspace that is not closed, using the
78-reciprocal function on $(0,1]$.

